  {
    question: "Can a used oil centrifuge separate glycol from engine oil?",
    answer:
      "An Alfa Laval used oil centrifuge can continuously separate glycol from used engine oil. The glycol will discharge through the centrifuge's heavy phase or water outlet.",
  },
  {
    question:
      "Is there a difference between a used oil centrifuge and WVO centrifuge?",
    answer:
      "A used oil centrifuge is designed to separate used oil from water and sludge. The same centrifuge may separate WVO from water and solids with some minor modifications. If the WVO application also involves biodiesel, the elastomers also need to be upgraded.",
  },
  {
    question:
      "How does acid-treated used oil affect the used oil centrifuge?",
    answer:
      "Acid-treated used oil has a low pH and is therefore not directly suitable for generic disc-stack centrifuges that may have material that is not compatible with low pH fluids. Specially manufactured acid-resistant components need to be retrofitted for such applications. Dolphin builds this as the DMPX-042 acid-service configuration, on a remanufactured Alfa Laval MOPX 209 or WHPX 410 self-cleaning separator.",
  },
  {
    question:
      "Can a centrifuge re-refine used oil into base oil?",
    answer:
      "No. Centrifuges do not re-refine used oil on their own. Re-refining to a Group II base oil needs vacuum distillation and hydrotreating to strip the light ends, additives, and heavy residue. What centrifuges do is the front end of a re-refinery: a decanter such as the Alfa Laval NX-314 pulls the bulk sludge out, and a high-speed disc-stack centrifuge such as the Alfa Laval MOPX 209 or WHPX 410 dehydrates and de-fuels the feed so the distillation column sees clean, dry oil. That protects the vacuum column and the hydrotreating catalyst, cuts energy load, and raises base oil yield. Processors who are not re-refining use the same two centrifuge stages on their own to produce a clean, dry recycled fuel oil.",
  },
  {
    question:
      "What does the centrifuge stage do for a used oil re-refinery?",
    answer:
      "It removes water, glycol, sediment, and metal wear particles before distillation. Free water flashing in a vacuum column wastes energy and upsets the column, and residual solids foul heat exchangers and poison hydrotreating catalyst. A two-stage centrifuge front end (decanter for desludging, disc-stack for water and fine solids) typically brings a used oil feed to under 0.1 percent water and sediment, which is the specification most re-refining and fuel-blending contracts are written against.",
  },
  {
    question:
      "What is the dryness of the separated sludge discharge of the decanter centrifuge?",
    answer:
      "The separated sludge discharge from a decanter centrifuge is typically about 50% dry. It commonly has a consistency of fudge or toothpaste. It is drip-free from well-adjusted decanters.",
  },
  {
    question:
      "Why can't we process used oil at ambient temperature?",
    answer:
      "Used oil at ambient temperature is quite viscous and resistant to separation in the centrifuge. Higher temperatures lower the viscosity of used oil, increasing the centrifuge efficiency by multitudes.",
  },
];

